Exactly. If you only value the destination, most of life becomes a waiting room.

The better skill is to make the process itself rewarding: curiosity during uncertainty, satisfaction in practice, meaning in effort, play during repetition, connection during the climb.

Then:

\text{journey} \neq \text{cost of arrival}

It becomes part of the payoff.

And strangely, that often improves the destination too. Someone who can enjoy iteration stays in the game longer, learns more, adapts faster, and is less shattered when the endpoint changes.

You still keep direction. You just stop requiring arrival before you allow yourself to feel alive.

Aim at the horizon. Live in the step.